# Check every text file out as LF on all platforms.
#
# The index already stores LF everywhere (verify with `git ls-files --eol`),
# so this changes nothing in history -- only what lands in the working tree.
# Without it, core.autocrlf=true on Windows checks the 11 tracked *.sh scripts
# out as CRLF, and bash then fails them with `$'\r': command not found`.
#
# No tracked file requires CRLF: there are no .bat/.cmd files in this repo.
# Binary files are left to git's own detection, which already classifies the
# tracked images correctly.
#
# After pulling this in, refresh your working tree once so the new attribute
# applies to files you already have:
#
#   git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard
#
* text=auto eol=lf
